---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../components/Container.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import ContentDocument from "../../components/ContentDocument.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About · Webxsid"
  description="A little context behind the work."
  seo={{
    title: "About · Webxsid",
    description: "A little context behind the work.",
    canonical: "/me",
  }}
  backHref="/"
>
  <Container>
    <div class="py-8 lg:py-14">
      <div class="max-w-4xl space-y-14 lg:space-y-16">
        <Section title="About me">
          <div class="space-y-7">
            <p
              class="max-w-3xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text sm:text-4xl lg:text-3xl lg:leading-[1.08]"
            >
              I build backend systems, developer tools, and software designed to
              stay useful over time.
            </p>

            <ContentDocument class="mt-2">
              <p>
                Most of my work lives somewhere between infrastructure,
                internal tooling, distributed systems, and developer
                experience. Right now I work at
                <a href="/work/taptic" class="inline-link"> Taptic </a>, where
                I’ve spent the last few years building APIs, automation
                systems, internal platforms, and the operational tooling around
                them.
              </p>

              <p>
                Before that I worked at

                <a href="/work/appyhigh" class="inline-link"> Appyhigh </a>,
                which was my first exposure to building production systems at
                scale and working closely with the kinds of operational
                problems that eventually pulled me deeper into backend
                engineering.
              </p>

              <p>
                A lot of that work involved simplifying systems that had slowly
                accumulated too much surface area — the kind of engineering
                work where reliability, clarity, and maintainability matter
                more than novelty.
              </p>

              <p>
                Outside of work, I spend most of my time building independent
                projects like
                <a href="/projects/crona" class="inline-link"> Crona </a>,
                experimenting with local-first software, and writing about
                systems, tooling, and engineering workflows.
              </p>
            </ContentDocument>
          </div>
        </Section>
        <Section title="How I work">
          <ContentDocument class="mt-2">
            <p>
              I tend to gravitate toward systems that remain understandable
              after time away from them. A lot of my engineering decisions come
              down to reducing unnecessary complexity, tightening feedback
              loops, and making operational behaviour easier to reason about.
            </p>

            <p>
              That usually pushes me toward smaller abstractions, direct
              workflows, and tooling that exposes what it is doing instead of
              hiding it behind layers of indirection. I enjoy the kind of
              software that becomes clearer the closer you get to it.
            </p>

            <p>
              Outside of product work, that thinking shows up most clearly in
              <a href="/projects" class="inline-link">
                the projects I build
              </a>, especially around local-first tooling and developer
              workflows. It also shapes the way I approach
              <a href="/writing" class="inline-link"> writing </a>
              — mostly as a way to document decisions, tradeoffs, and ideas while
              they are still forming.
            </p>

            <p>
              More recently I’ve been spending time around Linux, home
              infrastructure, and lower-level systems programming, partly out of
              curiosity and partly to better understand the layers modern
              software tends to abstract away.
            </p>
          </ContentDocument>
        </Section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</BaseLayout>
